{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-ledger", "file": "references/abstract-rule-vs-demo-heterogeneity.md", "content": "# Distinguish visual heterogeneity from task-rule drift

A fresh stream used demos with very different dimensions, palettes, backgrounds, and motifs from the test. The demos' observed edits were themselves disparate localized edits: demo 1 changed a pair of 5s to 6s; demo 2 changed a single 8 to 9; demo 3 changed a 7-cell to 9; demo 4 changed a local 7 region to 8; demo 5 changed two 7s to 3; demo 6 recolored a compact 2x3 patch from 2 to 5. No shared selector or abstract transformation was derived, and repeated requests were made without analyzing a cumulative delta ledger. The session's final candidate merely recolored the test's 8 structures to 4 without support from the demos.

Reusable inference lesson: neither assume that different-looking demos are unrelated nor force a common rule based on colors/salience. Precisely record each pair's delta, object context, and unchanged candidates; then seek measurable invariant relations (shape, relative position, local neighborhood, object role) across examples. Test any proposed rule by exact full-output replay on every demo. If deltas remain unlinked, further demos require a specific competing-hypothesis prediction; serial requests do not resolve uncertainty by themselves. For the test, keep its actual colors and structures unchanged unless a validated rule predicts edits.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484434/attempts/a51726d22484491ca3bb302d2d403d60/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-ledger/references/abstract-rule-vs-demo-heterogeneity.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-delta-discipline", "file": "references/compact-output-rejection-lesson.md", "content": "# Compact-output rejection: reassess representation

A trial on a partitioned grid with 6×4 cells separated by full-width divider rows produced a 3×3 demo output. The initial response incorrectly submitted a speculative 5×5 grid, effectively disregarding the demonstrated output scale and region structure. The next action requested another demo without articulating a discriminating prediction. On future tasks, an output whose dimensions are smaller than the input should immediately trigger compact region mapping: locate separators, count regions, associate regions to output locations, and test marker presence/color/count/position against all observed outputs. After an incorrect submission, reopen task class and output representation before trying again. Request further demos only after naming competing mappings and the specific observation that will distinguish them.

Note: the currently available demo in this case shows 11×14 input and 3×3 output, so infer only a 3×3 relation from it; do not assume a 6×3 output or transfer an unrelated example. The test input must be evaluated under the demonstrated output shape and its own divider structure.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484434/attempts/a51726d22484491ca3bb302d2d403d60/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-delta-discipline/references/compact-output-rejection-lesson.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-task-delta-inference", "file": "references/session-task-217fc4ad-review.md", "content": "# Session review: repeated unvalidated submissions after one demo

In this instance, the only demo had a 29×11 input and a 14×11 output, while the test was 4×9. The demo showed a dense structured field, a full-width separator row (color 4), and a uniform region below it; the output preserved the top 14×11 portion while changing its color-1 cells to color 2. This was strong evidence that the task was not a simple mapping of the test's visible 4×4 color-3 motif to a color-2 motif. The test also contained a full-height color-1 divider and color-3 regions, so its partition/representation needed to be inferred via a discriminating additional demo.

The unsuccessful actions repeatedly submitted variants of the same guessed 4×4 output, including an identical grid more than once, and claimed an overlap rule without demo support. After a wrong submission, reopen task class and output dimensions; do not repeat an identical grid or cosmetic variant. If the one demo does not establish how a differently sized test is represented, spend a demo request to resolve the mapping rather than burn attempts on unsupported guesses. Keep claims about the rule out of the response unless evidence validates them. In puzzle mode, still emit exactly one permitted JSON action on the final line.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484434/attempts/a51726d22484491ca3bb302d2d403d60/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-task-delta-inference/references/session-task-217fc4ad-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-incorrect-submission-recovery", "file": "references/path-selection-and-paint.md", "content": "# Separating path selection from recoloring

Straight-line or ray-filling tasks require two distinct inferences: (1) which endpoints/path cells are selected, and (2) which cells on that path change color. Do not assume that every aligned marker pair is connected, that only orthogonal or all eight directions are used, or that every intermediate cell is recolored. For each demonstration, record candidate alignments, intervening cells, old→new values, and aligned candidates that stayed unchanged. Then replay the full rule on immutable input copies and compare every output cell. A wrong result rejects the combined rule; revise endpoint selection and paint behavior as separate hypotheses rather than making cosmetic adjustments to the rendered grid.

Useful test matrix: endpoint color/identity; nearest versus any collinear partner; direction set; intervening-marker blocking; path termination; intermediate cells eligible by color; endpoint preservation; path overlaps. A candidate is ready only when these choices exactly reproduce all demos.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484434/attempts/a51726d22484491ca3bb302d2d403d60/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-incorrect-submission-recovery/references/path-selection-and-paint.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-incorrect-submission-recovery", "file": "references/continual-turn-lessons.md", "content": "# Continual ARC turn completeness and evidence boundaries

In continual puzzle interfaces, each message—including incorrect feedback—requires one legal action JSON on the final line. A thoughtful diagnosis without a valid action leaves the turn incomplete.

After a rejection, use references from previously encountered sessions only as process cautions. They are not evidence that this task shares the referenced transformation; do not transplant specific rules or guessed output grids. Reconstruct the current examples' deltas and representations, and demand exact replay of every available demo before another submission. If no supported candidate exists, request a demo only when it answers a concrete ambiguity that could change the output.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484434/attempts/a51726d22484491ca3bb302d2d403d60/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-incorrect-submission-recovery/references/continual-turn-lessons.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-stream-demo-discipline", "file": "references/failed-hypothesis-reset.md", "content": "# Resetting after failed ARC hypotheses

Repeated incorrect outputs indicate the task class or answer representation may be wrong, not merely a coordinate detail. Before spending another attempt, revisit all demonstrations: list input/output dimensions, identify separators and partitions, enumerate each region's candidate features, and test a compact region-summary mapping across every pair. Do not submit a revised pattern unless it exactly reproduces all known demonstrations. A new demo request should target a clearly stated unresolved distinction; serial requests without analysis impose cost without improving inference.

For small outputs from large partitioned canvases, prioritize understanding the relationship between each cell/region and the compact answer. Keep a candidate mapping table and explicitly account for exceptions (e.g. output cells retaining a background/category color). Only after the mapping reproduces all demos should it be applied to the test.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484434/attempts/a51726d22484491ca3bb302d2d403d60/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-stream-demo-discipline/references/failed-hypothesis-reset.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-delta-discipline", "file": "references/panel-count-encoding.md", "content": "# Panel marker-count encoding: diagnostic and validation lesson

This instance had grids partitioned by full separator bands and compact outputs shaped like the panel array. Several demos were consistent with an output marker for panels containing multiple instances of the designated non-background color; others had different marker colors and output backgrounds. A test answer based on a guessed threshold and manually counted panels was rejected, so the evidence does not establish the exact test encoding or make a reusable color/threshold rule.

Reusable method: transcribe the grid exactly; locate separator rows/columns; define half-open panel bounds; count target-color cells in every panel; build a table of panel coordinates, counts, and corresponding output values. Test competing count mappings (including exact count versus threshold) against *all* demonstrations, especially panels with zero, one, two, and three-or-more markers. Confirm orientation, output dimensions, background encoding, and values separately. After rejection, first independently re-check serialization and the panel count table; do not repeat a merely reformatted candidate. Do not claim a count rule until full demo replay supports it.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484434/attempts/a51726d22484491ca3bb302d2d403d60/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-delta-discipline/references/panel-count-encoding.md"}

