The stream included edge bars and isolated/motif colors, but the sole supplied demo that changed cells changed only two adjacent 5s to 6s (demo 1: rows 2–3, column 1 in zero-based coordinates); demos 2–4 changed only small portions: demo 2 changed three 7s in a 7-bar to 4s, demo 3 changed a 3x2 patch of 8s to 7s, and demo 4 recolored a compact 5-shape to 6. These are distinct local recolorings, not evidence for projecting broad edge rays. Repeated submissions that altered a guessed 3-color edge pattern failed, illustrating the need to fit a selector or relation to all demos before transfer.

Operational lesson: compute each demo's exact delta, including changed old/new colors and unchanged candidates; do not infer common mechanics just from edge bars or marker presence. If hypotheses remain unvalidated, a demo is useful only if its geometry discriminates them. Do not submit a rule unsupported by exact demo replay. This note does not establish a solved transformation for this task.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484434/attempts/a51726d22484491ca3bb302d2d403d60/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-task-delta-inference/references/session-dabb45d0-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-ledger", "file": "references/session-task-7db5e3d5-review.md", "content": "# Review: heterogeneous demonstrations without a validated common rule

This instance supplied three sharply different pairs: a 21×21 sparse diagonal-color scene became a full 5-color periodic field; a 23×23 otherwise blank grid with two bottom stripes became an alternating two-color row pattern; and a 2×2 near-uniform patch had its lone zero filled. The 12×12 test instead contained offset diagonal tracks in colors 8, 2, 6, and 7. The demonstrations were evidence of radically different surface transformations, but no cumulative exact-delta analysis produced a validated shared selector or output mapping. Serial demo requests (three were used) did not resolve the relationship; the last action continued requesting rather than making an evidence-based decision.

Reusable lesson: task IDs and stream membership alone do not justify assuming a common pixel-level operation. Record each pair's exact delta and output structure before requesting another. Explicitly test whether the task may be an abstract transformation shared across different visual classes; formulate at least two candidate abstractions and derive predictions that can be replayed against all examples. If no candidate maps all demonstrations exactly, another request is justified only if its predicted outcomes discriminate those candidates. Do not invent a test output by blending the demo patterns or select one demo's operation based on superficial resemblance. Keep the action protocol strict: one JSON action only on the final line.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792484434/attempts/a51726d22484491ca3bb302d2d403d60/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-ledger/references/session-task-7db5e3d5-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-ledger", "file": "references/failed-guess-reassessment.md", "content": "# Failed-submission reassessment

When a plausible full-grid cleanup or marker-repair guess is incorrect, treat that as evidence against the whole inferred rule, not a cue to make another nearby edit. Reconstruct the exact input/output delta in every demo, including unchanged markers and structures. Separate the observed operation from assumptions about color regions and object boundaries. If multiple rules still fit, name them and request a demo specifically predicted to differ; otherwise submit only a transformation replayed against all available examples. This avoids cascading unsupported guesses and needless penalties.
